<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Network security posture (F12, ADR-0016).

  Two things this declares that the platform would otherwise decide for us — and "a default is not
  a decision" is the whole reason the file exists:

  1. cleartextTrafficPermitted="false". targetSdk 36 already denies cleartext, but the platform
     default is computed from the OS version's own rules, and on API 26/27 devices — which minSdk
     26 puts in our install base — the default inverts to PERMITTED. Declaring it makes the posture
     the same sentence on every device the app can run on.

  2. trust-anchors: system certificates ONLY. No <certificates src="user" />. A CA installed by an
     employer's MDM, by malware, or by a curious user cannot intercept this app's TLS.

  NO <debug-overrides> ON PURPOSE, and the cost is real: nobody can inspect this app's traffic with
  Charles, mitmproxy or Burp on ANY build, because a proxy's CA lands in the user store and the
  user store is not trusted here. That is the correct trade for an app whose traffic is a patient
  agenda. If the author ever wants proxy debugging, adding <debug-overrides> is a deliberate change
  to this file with its own note — not something a slice does in passing.

  Careful: a file named network_security_config_debug.xml next to this one would be auto-discovered
  and applied in debuggable builds. Scripts/check-network-posture.sh refuses its existence.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
